['Here is the basic code structure for your Java service. Due to space limitations and complexity, this sample includes only the main components and doesn't cover all detailed requirements. It should serve as a good starting point for you to build upon. 

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import org.json.*;

public class PokemonService {

    private static final String CONFIG_URL = "http://pokemonservice.com/config.json";
    private static final long CACHE_EXPIRATION = 10 * 60 * 1000; // Cache expires after 10 minutes

    private JSONObject cachedConfig;
    private long lastCacheUpdateTime;

    public PokemonService() {
        fetchAndCacheConfiguration();
    }

    private synchronized void fetchAndCacheConfiguration() {
        try {
            URL url = new URL(CONFIG_URL);

            HttpURLConnection connection = (HttpURLConnection) url.openConnection();
            connection.setRequestMethod("GET");

            int statusCode = connection.getResponseCode();

            if (statusCode == 200) {
                BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(connection.getInputStream()));

                String inputLine;
                StringBuilder content = new StringBuilder();

                while ((inputLine = in.readLine()) != null) {
                    content.append(inputLine);
                }

                in.close();

                cachedConfig = new JSONObject(content.toString());
                lastCacheUpdateTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
            } else {
                throw new IOException("HTTP error code: " + statusCode);
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            // Log and handle the exception here
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    public JSONObject getPokemonData(String pokemonName) {
        long currentTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

        if (cachedConfig == null || currentTime - lastCacheUpdateTime > CACHE_EXPIRATION) {
            fetchAndCacheConfiguration(); // Refetch the configuration if it has expired or is not yet fetched
        }

        JSONObject pokemonData = cachedConfig.getJSONObject(pokemonName);

        return pokemonData;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PokemonService service = new PokemonService();

        String pokemonToFetch = "pikachu";
        JSONObject data = service.getPokemonData(pokemonToFetch);

        System.out.println("Data for " + pokemonToFetch + ": " + data.toString());
    }
}
``` 
This code provides a basic example of how you might handle caching and retrieving data from an external API in Java. Error handling, logging, performance optimization, concurrency management, integration with external systems/APIs, testing, monitoring, and feedback loops are more complex topics that would require much more extensive coding and wouldn't be covered here.
']

['This task is quite complex to fully implement here due to its depth and size, but I can provide you with an outline for how it might look like:

```C
#include <dlfcn.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <pthread.h>

// Structure to hold the cryptographic algorithm's details
typedef struct {
    char *name;
    void *handle;
} AlgorithmDetails;

// Global list of registered algorithms
static AlgorithmDetails **algorithms = NULL;
static size_t numAlgorithms = 0;

// Mutex for thread safety
static pthread_mutex_t lock;

// Loads a cryptographic algorithm
bool loadAlgorithm(const char *path) {
    // Lock to ensure safe writes and reads
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    // Allocate more memory if necessary
    if (numAlgorithms % 10 == 0) {
        algorithms = realloc(algorithms, sizeof(*algorithms) * (numAlgorithms + 10));
    }

    // Load the shared object
    void *handle = dlopen(path, RTLD_LAZY);

    if (!handle) {
        printf("Failed to load algorithm: %s\n", dlerror());
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
        return false;
    }

    // Get the name of the loaded algorithm
    char *(*getName)(void) = dlsym(handle, "getAlgorithmName");

    if (!getName) {
        printf("Failed to get algorithm name: %s\n", dlerror());
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
        return false;
    }

    // Add the loaded algorithm to the list of algorithms
    AlgorithmDetails *algorithm = malloc(sizeof(*algorithm));
    algorithm->name = strdup(getName());
    algorithm->handle = handle;

    algorithms[numAlgorithms++] = algorithm;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

    return true;
}

// Executes a cryptographic algorithm
void *executeAlgorithm(const char *name, void *data) {
    // Lock to ensure safe writes and reads
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    // Find the algorithm in our list of algorithms
    for (size_t i = 0; i < numAlgorithms; ++i) {
        if (!strcmp(algorithms[i]->name, name)) {
            void *(*execute)(void *) = dlsym(algorithms[i]->handle, "execute");

            if (!execute) {
                printf("Failed to execute algorithm: %s\n", dlerror());
                pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
                return NULL;
            }

            void *result = execute(data);

            pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

            return result;
        }
    }

    printf("Algorithm not found: %s\n", name);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

    return NULL;
}
```
This is a basic outline and does not take into account all the mentioned challenges. For example, it doesn't include garbage collection, error handling, or performance optimization mechanisms like thread safety or latency reduction. Also, memory management is kept to a minimum for brevity. Depending on how you want to handle these aspects, there can be many ways of implementing them within this framework.
']

