['Here's a simplified example for the given problem. Please note that this is just a basic skeleton and does not cover all constraints mentioned above. Implementing such a complex function would involve significant development effort and would require more details about the specific requirements and third-party build tool you are working with. 

```rust
use std::{process, time};

fn compile_binary(env: &str) -> Result<(), String> {
    let start = time::Instant::now();

    // Initialize logger
    let log_file = std::fs::File::create("log.txt").unwrap();
    let mut builder = process::Command::new(env);

    builder.stdout(process::Stdio::piped())
           .stderr(log_file)
           // Add other build tool configuration here
           ;

    match builder.spawn() {
        Ok(mut child) => {
            let output = match child.wait_with_output() {
                Ok(result) => result,
                Err(e) => return Err(format![
                    "Failed to wait for process: {}", e]),
            };

            if !output.status.success() {
                return Err("Build failed".to_string());
            } else {
                let elapsed = start.elapsed();
                println![
                    "Compilation finished in {} seconds.",
                    elapsed.as_secs(),
                ];

                // Handle memory usage here

                Ok(())
            }
        }
        Err(e) => return Err(format![
            "Failed to spawn process: {}", e]),
    }
}
```
This function sets up the build environment using a third-party tool specified by env. It captures the system time at the start of the compilation process, tracks any potential error during the setup or execution process, and then calculates elapsed time for performance measurement. 

