This is a basic overview of how you might organize your library. It includes OpenSSL libraries for MD5 and SHA256 hashing, jansson for JSON parsing, as well as some general function prototypes. 

Here's an example of a `process_file` function that reads the file in chunks (you would have to adjust this depending on your specific requirements):
```C
#include <stdio.h>
#define BUFFER_SIZE 1024 // Or whatever chunk size you decide on
void process_file(const char *filename, unsigned int (*func)(const char *)) {
    FILE* file = fopen(filename, "rb");
    if (file == NULL) handle_errors(); // Replace with your error handling logic

    unsigned char buffer[BUFFER_SIZE];
    size_t bytes_read = 0;
    while ((bytes_read = fread(buffer, 1, BUFFER_SIZE, file)) > 0) {
        func(buffer); // Apply hash function to the chunk of data. Note that this will have to be adjusted depending on your specific requirements for the MD5 and SHA256 functions
    }
}
```
The `handle_errors` function should contain appropriate error handling logic, such as logging errors or raising exceptions. 

The `validate_token` function can use jansson's JSON parsing functionality to check the required fields:

```C
bool validate_token(const char *json_string) {
    json_error_t error;
    json_t* root = json_loads(json_string, 0, &error);

    json_t* access_token = json_object_get(root, "access_token");
    json_t* expires_in = json_object_get(root, "expires_in");
    json_t* token_type = json_object_get(root, "token_type");

    if (!json_is_string(access_token) || !json_is_integer(expires_in) || !json_is_string(token_type)) {
        return false;
    }

    if (strcmp(json_string_value(token_type), "Bearer") != 0 || json_integer_value(expires_in) <= 0) {
        return false;
    }

    // Optional field, check if it exists and is a string
    json_t* refresh_token = json_object_get(root, "refresh_token");
    if (refresh_token != NULL && !json_is_string(refresh_token)) {
        return false;
    }

    // If we made it this far without returning, the token is valid
    return true;
}
```
This should give you a good starting point for creating your library. Remember to add comments and documentation throughout your code to make it easy for others to understand what's going on. Also remember to test all of your functions thoroughly using unit tests to ensure they work as expected.
']

