```cpp
// This is a simple pseudo-code for demonstration purposes only
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <memory>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>

class DataSource {
public:
    virtual std::vector<int> getData() = 0;
};

class RealTimeDataSource : public DataSource {
public:
    // Implement your own methods for real-time data sources here.
};

class BatchDataSource : public DataSource {
public:
    // Implement your own methods for batch data sources here.
};

// Manager class to manage external data sources
class DataManager {
private:
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<DataSource>> dataSources;
    std::mutex mtx;  // For thread safety
public:
    void addDataSource(std::unique_ptr<DataSource> source) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        dataSources.push_back(std::move(source));
    }

    std::vector<int> getAllData() {
        std::vector<int> allData;
        std::vector<std::thread> threads;

        for (auto& source : dataSources) {
            // Start a new thread to process each data source independently
            threads.push_back(std::thread([&]() {
                auto data = source->getData();

                std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);  // Lock the mutex before writing to allData
                allData.insert(allData.end(), data.begin(), data.end());
            }));
        }

        for (auto& thread : threads) {
            thread.join();  // Wait for all threads to finish
        }

        return allData;
    }
};
```
In this pseudo code, we create an abstract class `DataSource` and two classes that inherit from it: `RealTimeDataSource` and `BatchDataSource`, which represent real-time data sources and batch-oriented data sources respectively. 

We define a manager class (`DataManager`) that holds all the `dataSources` and manages them concurrently using threads. Each thread retrieves data from its respective source. The `mtx` mutex is used to prevent race conditions when writing to `allData` vector. 

Please note that this is a simplified demonstration. In a real-world scenario, you would also need to handle errors and exceptions (try/catch blocks), implement encryption, rate limiting, batch processing, secure data transmission and storage, concurrent processing, etc. This will involve more complex design patterns and libraries for handling external dependencies like Boost Asio or libcurl for networking.
