['Implementing an entire e-commerce platform from scratch is beyond the scope of this assistant but I can provide a simple example how you might change file extension using sprintf and strcat functions in C. It takes original filename, appends a numeric identifier and file type indicator to it.

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

void ChangeFileExtension(char *fileName, char *newExt, int id) {
    // Get the length of original filename
    size_t len = strlen(fileName);

    // Create buffer for new filename
    char newFileName[50];

    // Copy original filename to new filename buffer
    strcpy(newFileName, fileName);

    // Get index of last dot in filename (before extension)
    int dotIndex = -1;
    for (int i=len-1; i>=0; --i) {
        if (fileName[i] == '.') {
            dotIndex = i;
            break;
        }
    }

    // If no dot found, append new extension to the end of filename
    if(dotIndex < 0){
      sprintf(newFileName+len,".%s",newExt);
    }else{
      // Otherwise, replace original extension with new one
      sprintf(newFileName+dotIndex, ".%d.%s", id, newExt);
    }

    printf("New File Name: %s\n", newFileName);
}
```
This is a simplified example and might not cover all edge cases but it should give you an idea how to solve the task at hand. In production code, consider adding error checking and validation for different scenarios as well. 

Note: This code only changes the extension of filename, if file has no extension or other special cases it may need adjustment. For instance, this will not work with files that have multiple dots in them (like "file.name.txt") as it just takes last dot as extension start marker. Also, it's important to remember the limit on buffer size you've set and ensure it's large enough for new filename string.
']

