['Here is a simplified version of how your program could look like. This code won't run as expected since it doesn't include all the requirements (like specific server health monitoring or error handling), but I hope you can get an idea about the structure from here. 

Please note that creating such a system requires a deep understanding of networking, concurrency, performance optimization and C++ programming. The provided code is just a starting point and should be extended according to your requirements.

```cpp
#include <boost/asio.hpp>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

using boost::asio::ip::tcp;

class SandeshClient {
public:
    SandeshClient(const std::vector<std::string>& servers) 
        : servers_(servers), io_service_(), socket_(io_service_) {}

    void get(const std::string& path) {
        boost::asio::connect(socket_, tcp::resolver(io_service_).resolve({ "localhost", "80" }));

        // Send request to server.
        size_t request_length = std::strlen("GET ") + path.size() + 1;
        boost::asio::write(socket_, boost::asio::buffer("GET ", 4));
        boost::asio::write(socket_, boost::asio::buffer(path));
        boost::asio::write(socket_, boost::asio::buffer("\n", 1));

        // Read response.
        std::string response;
        for (;;) {
            char header[256];
            size_t bytes_read = socket_.read_some(boost::asio::buffer(header, 256), ec);
            if (ec == boost::asio::error::eof) break; // Connection closed cleanly by peer.
            else if (ec) throw boost::system::system_error(ec); // Some other error.

            response += std::string(header, bytes_read);
        }

        std::cout << "Response: " << response << "\n";
    }
private:
    boost::asio::io_service io_service_;
    tcp::socket socket_;

    // For simplicity the servers are just hostnames. In a real-world scenario this could be an IP and port tuple.
    std::vector<std::string> servers_; 
};

int main() {
    try {
        SandeshClient client({ "server1", "server2" });

        // Send GET requests asynchronously to different servers.
        for (const auto& server : { "server1", "path1" }) {
            std::thread t([&client, &server](){ client.get(server); });
            t.detach();
        }
    } catch (std::exception& e) {
        std::cerr << "Exception: " << e.what() << "\n";
    }

    return 0;
}
```
The `SandeshClient` class has a simple get method which connects to the server and sends a GET request for a given path. The connection is kept open and reused for subsequent requests, which is a common practice in HTTP/1.1 due to its connection pooling mechanism. 

Please note this is a simplistic version of what you're asking for. In production code, you would need to add much more complexity such as load balancing strategies, error handling, health monitoring etc. Also, using raw pointers in modern C++ would be best replaced by smart pointers or std::optional/std::expected types where applicable.
