This code is quite simplified and lacks error handling, concurrency control, integration with an external Key Management Service etc. It's just to give you a starting point of how the library can be structured:

```c
#include <openssl/aes.h>
#include <string.h>

/* Simplified key retrieval from KMS */
char *get_key() {
    // Retrieve key from external KMS 
    char *key = malloc(16);
    memset(key, 'A', 16);
    return key;
}

void AES_encrypt(const unsigned char *in, unsigned char *out) {
    AES_KEY aesKey;
    char *key = get_key();

    /* Key setup for encryption */
    if (AES_set_encrypt_key((unsigned char*)key, 128, &aesKey) < 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to set AES key for encryption\n");
        return;
    }

    /* Encryption */
    AES_encrypt(in, out, &aesKey);
}

void AES_decrypt(const unsigned char *in, unsigned char *out) {
    AES_KEY aesKey;
    char *key = get_key();

    /* Key setup for decryption */
    if (AES_set_decrypt_key((unsigned char*)key, 128, &aesKey) < 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to set AES key for decryption\n");
        return;
    }

    /* Decryption */
    AES_decrypt(in, out, &aesKey);
}
```

This code snippet only supports symmetric encryption using the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) in ECB mode with a static key. For other modes like CTR or GCM, you would need to use `EVP_CipherInit_ex` and `EVP_CIPHER_CTX` functions from OpenSSL's EVP APIs. 

For comprehensive cryptographic operations including asymmetric encryption and digital signatures, integration with an external Key Management Service, unit tests, logging, error handling, multi-threading support and performance optimization you would need to extend this code in a more detailed way or use existing libraries that have been developed by experts. You should also review industry best practices for secure coding and cryptographic standards like NIST SP 800-57.
