['Here is an example of how you could implement `sendEmailVerificationToken` in PHP, it doesn't cover all use cases mentioned as some aspects can be complex to implement. 

```php
<?php

require 'vendor/autoload.php';
use PhpAmqpLib\Connection\AMQPStreamConnection;
use PhpAmqpLib\Message\AMQPMessage;

class EmailVerificationService {
    private $connection;
    private $channel;

    public function __construct() {
        $this->connection = new AMQPStreamConnection('localhost', 5672, 'guest', 'guest');
        $this->channel = $this->connection->channel();

        $this->channel->queue_declare('email_verification_queue', false, true, false, false);
    }

    public function __destruct() {
        $this->channel->close();
        $this->connection->close();
    }

    public function sendEmailVerificationToken($userId) {
        // Generate a unique token for the user. 
        $token = bin2hex(random_bytes(16));

        // Store the token in Redis or Memcached for faster access and to handle server restarts.
        try {
            $redis = new \Redis();
            $redis->connect('127.0.0.1', 6379);
            $redis->setex($token, 86400, $userId); // Token expires in 24 hours (86400 seconds)
        } catch (\RedisException $e) {
            error_log("Error connecting to Redis: " . $e->getMessage());
            return false;
        }

        $msg = new AMQPMessage(json_encode([
            'userId' => $userId,
            'token'  => $token
        ]));

        $this->channel->basic_publish($msg, '', 'email_verification_queue');
    }
}
```
Please note that this is a simplified version and does not cover all aspects of your request. This example assumes you have set up RabbitMQ for sending messages and Redis for storing tokens. Also, error handling and fallback mechanisms are very basic in this example. Real-world applications would need more complex error handling and recovery strategies.
