['Here's how you can create the logging functionality with C++11 features:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <cstdarg>
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>

static std::mutex logMutex;

void Log(const char *file, int line, const char* condition, const char* fmt...) {
    if (!condition) return; // no logging if the given condition is NULL or evaluates to false

    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(logMutex);
    va_list argList;
    int maxSize = 1024, requiredSize;
    char* buffer = new char[maxSize];
    while (true) {
        va_start(argList, fmt);
        requiredSize = vsnprintf(buffer, maxSize, fmt, argList);
        va_end(argList);

        if (requiredSize < 0) return; // encoding error occurred
        else if (requiredSize < maxSize) break; // successfully written the formatted string into the buffer

        maxSize = requiredSize + 1; // make room for null character
        delete[] buffer;
        buffer = new char[maxSize];
    }

    std::cerr << "Error at " << file << ':' << line << ": " << buffer << '\n';
    delete[] buffer;
}
```
This function `Log` creates a message from the given format and arguments, but only if the condition is not null. It ensures that logging is thread-safe using a mutex. It also dynamically adjusts the size of the buffer to hold the formatted string according to the result returned by `vsnprintf`.

To use this function, you can define your own macro:
```cpp
#define LOG(condition, fmt...) Log(__FILE__, __LINE__, #condition, ##fmt)
```
Then, whenever you want to log a message, you can do:
```cpp
LOG(some_condition, "The condition %s was not met", some_condition);
```
This will only log the message if `some_condition` is not null or evaluates to false. Note that `##fmt` is used in the macro definition to handle zero arguments (i.e., when no format string and arguments are provided). 

Please note that you need a C++11-compliant compiler for this code. Also, be aware of the potential performance implications of using variable argument lists: they can cause unnecessary memory allocation if used incorrectly. In this case, however, all variables captured by `va_start` will not outlive the function call.
']

