['Below is a simplified version of such an implementation in PHP using MySQL and RabbitMQ. Note this doesn't cover all operational challenges mentioned, it merely provides an idea of how to approach this problem:

```php
<?php
require_once __DIR__ . '/vendor/autoload.php';
use PhpAmqpLib\Connection\AMQPStreamConnection;
use PhpAmqpLib\Message\AMQPMessage;

function sendEmailVerificationToken($email, $token) {
    // Generate verification token and store in DB with expiration time. 
    $expireTime = date('Y-m-d H:i:s', strtotime('+24 hours'));

    // Connect to MySQL Database and store the token details.
    $mysqli = new mysqli("localhost", "my_user", "my_password", "world");

    $stmt = $mysqli->prepare("INSERT INTO tokens (email, token, expireTime) VALUES (?, ?, ?)");
    $stmt->bind_param('sss', $email, $token, $expireTime);
    $stmt->execute();
    $stmt->close();

    // Send a message to RabbitMQ queue. 
    $connection = new AMQPStreamConnection('localhost', 5672, 'guest', 'guest');
    $channel = $connection->channel();
    $msg = new AMQPMessage(json_encode(['email' => $email, 'token' => $token]));
    $channel->basic_publish($msg, '', 'verificationEmailQueue');

    echo " [x] Sent verification email to $email with token: $token\n";
    $channel->close();
    $connection->close();
}
?>
```
This is a simplified example, the actual implementation would involve more detailed error handling and data validation. It also requires further configuration of MySQL connection details and RabbitMQ broker. For managing multiple third-party APIs fallbacks and dynamic routing policies, PHP's APCu cache can be used to store API health metrics for each provider.
