['Here's a basic skeleton of how you might implement this in C using POSIX threads for threading and mutexes for synchronization. 

Please note that the actual implementation details would heavily depend on the specifics of your external data source interface, which is not specified in the problem statement. Also, please be aware that handling network latency, potential data loss, error handling, and so forth are complex tasks beyond this skeleton's scope.

```C
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdio.h>

typedef struct {
    int sum;
    pthread_mutex_t mutex;
} SharedData;

void *processStream(void *arg) {
    SharedData *data = (SharedData *)arg;
    int input;

    while (scanf("%d", &input) == 1) {
        if (input % 2 == 0) {
            pthread_mutex_lock(&(data->mutex));
            data->sum += input;
            printf("Current sum: %d\n", data->sum);
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&(data->mutex));
        }
    }

    return NULL;
}

void resetState(SharedData *data) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&(data->mutex));
    data->sum = 0;
    printf("State has been reset\n");
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&(data->mutex));
}

int main() {
    SharedData data = { .sum = 0, .mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER };

    pthread_t thread;
    if (pthread_create(&thread, NULL, processStream, &data) != 0) {
        perror("Failed to create a new thread");
        return 1;
    }

    // Main program logic here...

    resetState(&data);

    if (pthread_join(thread, NULL) != 0) {
        perror("Failed to join the thread");
        return 1;
    }

    pthread_mutex_destroy(&(data.mutex));
    return 0;
}
```

The main program logic would be where you'd actually read from your external data source, handle errors and other issues (which are not implemented in this skeleton). The `processStream` function reads integers from standard input (you could replace it with a function that connects to your data source and fetches the stream) and updates the shared sum if the integer is even. It uses a mutex to ensure thread-safe access to the shared sum variable.

The `resetState` function resets the state by setting the sum back to 0, and it also locks the mutex before doing so to ensure it's done atomically. The main program creates a new thread running `processStream`, waits for user input to reset the state, then joins with the processing thread before cleaning up.
']

