To implement the filename manipulation function you asked for:

```csharp
public string AppendIdentifierAndTypeIndicator(string originalName, long identifier, string fileType) {
    // Check if original name is too long to prevent buffer overflow
    if (originalName.Length > 1024) {
        throw new ArgumentException("Original filename is too long");
    }

    // Construct the new filename securely
    var newFileName = string.Format("{0}_{1}.{2}", originalName, identifier, fileType);

    return newFileName;
}
```
This function takes an original filename and appends a numeric identifier and a type indicator to it. It uses `string.Format` for this purpose which is safer than using unchecked string concatenation methods like `+` or `sprintf` in C/C++ because it ensures buffer overflows cannot happen due to fixed size of newFileName variable.

Please note that this function does not handle filenames with special characters properly, you would need additional logic to escape such characters if necessary. Also, I have kept the file name length limit as 1024 bytes which is a very arbitrary value and can be adjusted based on your specific requirements. 

