The corresponding C++ source file `CombatActions.cpp` will contain the implementation of your methods. Here's a simple example of how you can implement insertion and update:

```cpp
// CombatActions.cpp
#include "CombatActions.h"
#include <algorithm>

namespace CombatActions {
    void ActionSequence::insertAfterOrAppend(const std::string& target, const std::string& action) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx); // Ensure thread safety.

        auto it = std::find(actions.begin(), actions.end(), target);

        if (it != actions.end()) 
            actions.insert_after(it, action); // Insert after the target string.
        else 
            actions.push_front(action); // If not found, append to the front.
    }

    void ActionSequence::updateAction(const std::string& oldAction, const std::string& newAction) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx); // Ensure thread safety.

        std::replace(actions.begin(), actions.end(), oldAction, newAction); // Replace the old action with the new one.
    }

    bool ActionSequence::deleteAction(const std::string& actionToDelete) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx); // Ensure thread safety.

        auto it = std::find(actions.begin(), actions.end(), actionToDelete);

        if (it != actions.end()) { 
            actions.remove(actionToDelete); // Remove the action to delete.
            return true; // Indicate that deletion was successful.
        } else 
            return false; // Return false if action not found.
    }
}
```

The library design provided here is just a starting point and does not take into account all of your requirements but it gives you an idea of how to start implementing such a complex system. To fully implement your library, you need to consider various other factors like error handling, integration with external APIs, unit tests etc. which are beyond the scope of this platform.
