['This is a complex task that requires multiple layers of programming. Here's an outline on how you might approach it in Java using Spring Boot for microservices, Resilience4j for fault tolerance features, and the Netflix Hystrix library for circuit breaking strategies.

```java
import org.springframework.cloud.client.circuitbreaker.EnableCircuitBreaker;
import io.github.resilience4j.retry.annotation.Retry;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import com.netflix.hystrix.contrib.javanica.annotation.HystrixCommand;

@EnableCircuitBreaker
public class ApiIntegrationService {

    @Autowired
    private RestTemplate restTemplate;

    // Integrate with a payment processing system, handling transactions securely and ensuring accurate fund reflections
    @PostMapping("/transaction")
    @Retry(name = "default", fallbackMethod = "fallbackMethod")
    public ResponseEntity<String> handleTransaction(@RequestBody Transaction transaction) {
        String uri = "http://payment-processing-system/transactions";

        // Process and forward transaction securely
        restTemplate.postForObject(uri, transaction, String.class);
    }

    public ResponseEntity<String> fallbackMethod(Transaction transaction, Throwable t) {
        return new ResponseEntity<>("Fallback method executed: " + t.getMessage(), HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR);
    }
}
```

This is just a simple example of how you might integrate with an external API and add fault tolerance to your service using Resilience4j's @Retry annotation for retries with exponential backoff and the fallback method for handling errors. 

