['A direct solution cannot be provided because you have listed a lot of sub-tasks and implementation details which are generally specific to the application context and require extensive architecture design, integration with various systems (like cloud infrastructure for configuration management and logging), and optimization techniques. 

However, I can provide an example of how one could start such a module in Rust using tokio and notify library to monitor changes in external config file. Please note that this is only a skeleton code and not a full-fledged solution:

```rust
use std::sync::Arc;
use futures::stream::StreamExt;
use notify::{watcher, RecursiveMode, Watcher};
use tokio::sync::RwLock;
use std::time::Duration;
use tokio::time::interval;
use anyhow::Result;
use std::fs::OpenOptions;
use serde_json::Value as JsonValue;

pub struct ConfigManager {
    watcher: Watcher,
    config_path: String,
    cache: Arc<RwLock<Option<JsonValue>>>,
}

impl ConfigManager {
    pub fn new(config_path: &str) -> Result<Self> {
        let (tx, rx) = tokio::sync::mpsc::channel(10);
        let watcher = watcher(tx)?;

        Ok(ConfigManager {
            watcher,
            config_path: String::from(config_path),
            cache: Arc::new(RwLock::new(None)),
        })
    }

    pub async fn run(&self) {
        self.watcher.watch(self.config_path.clone(), RecursiveMode::Recursive).unwrap();
        let mut stream = interval(Duration::from_secs(10));

        loop {
            stream.tick().await;

            if let Some((_, event)) = self.watcher.recv().await {
                match event {
                    notify::DebouncedEvent::Modify(_) => {
                        tokio::spawn(async move {
                            if let Ok(config) = self.load_config() {
                                *self.cache.write().await = Some(config);
                            }
                        });
                    },
                    _ => (),
                }
            }
        }
    }

    async fn load_config(&self) -> Result<JsonValue> {
        let file = OpenOptions::new()
            .read(true)
            .open(&self.config_path)?;

        Ok(serde_json::from_reader(file)?)
    }

    pub async fn get_config(&self) -> Option<JsonValue> {
        let cache = self.cache.read().await;
        *cache
    }
}
``` 

In this code, we start a `Watcher` on the specified config path and wait for changes. When a change is detected, we reload the configuration and update our cached value. You would need to extend this to handle multiple services trying to modify the configuration at once (using a distributed locking mechanism like etcd or Zookeeper), integrate with logging services, etc.
']

