2) Thread safety for a Cache using `std::unordered_map` (C++ Standard Library). This map is thread-safe as of C++11.

```cpp
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <string>
#include <optional>

class ThumbnailCache {
public:
    void insert(const std::string& key, const ImageData& image) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mMutex);
        mMap[key] = image;
    }

    std::optional<ImageData> get(const std::string& key) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mMutex);
        auto it = mMap.find(key);
        if (it != mMap.end()) return it->second;
        else return {};
    }

private:
    std::unordered_map<std::string, ImageData> mMap;
    std::mutex mMutex;
};
```

3) Handling Exponential Backoff and Jitter in retrying failed API requests. This is a very basic implementation of exponential backoff:

```cpp
#include <thread>
#include <random>

// Assuming cpr::Get returns the response status code directly
int attempt_api_request(const std::string& url, int maxRetries) {
    int delay = 1; // Initial delay of 1 second

    for (int i = 0; i < maxRetries; ++i) {
        int status = cpr::Get(cpr::Url{url});

        if (status == 200) return 200;

        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(delay));
        delay *= 2; // Double the delay for each retry
    }

    return -1; // Return -1 if we hit max retries and still failed to connect
}
```
This is a very basic example and real-world code would need to be more robust in terms of error handling, timeouts, rate limits etc. This code also does not include the "jitter" part of an exponential backoff strategy which can help avoid synchronization issues when multiple clients start retrying at the same time.
']

