['Below is an example of how you could implement this solution using Spring Boot, Redis, Kafka, Resilience4j (for circuit breaker pattern), Spring Security for OAuth 2.0 and rate limiting etc. However, this is a high-level overview and actual implementation requires detailed planning, design, and development in a team environment considering multiple factors such as security considerations, performance optimization, reliability and error handling strategies, user experience considerations etc.

```java
@SpringBootApplication
@EnableCaching
public class RestApiApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(RestApiApplication.class, args);
    }
}

@Service
public class ExternalServiceIntegrator {
    private final RetryRegistry retryRegistry;
    private final CircuitBreaker circuitBreaker;

    @Autowired
    public ExternalServiceIntegrator(RetryRegistry retryRegistry, CircuitBreakerFactory circuitBreakerFactory) {
        this.retryRegistry = retryRegistry;
        this.circuitBreaker = circuitBreakerFactory.create("externalService");
    }

    @Retry(name="externalService", fallbackMethod="handleExternalServiceFailure")
    public Object callExternalService() {
        // Call the external service here, return result
    }

    private Object handleExternalServiceFailure(Exception ex) throws Exception {
        if (circuitBreaker.allowsExecution()) {
            try {
                Retry retry = retryRegistry.retry("externalService");
                return retry.executeSupplier(() -> callExternalService());
            } catch (RetryException e) {
                throw new ExternalServiceException("Failed to connect to external service", ex);
            } finally {
                circuitBreaker.onSuccess(0, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
            }
        } else {
            throw new ExternalServiceException("Circuit breaker is open for external service", ex);
        }
    }
}
```
The above example demonstrates the use of Resilience4J to manage failures when integrating with external services. The `@Retry` annotation allows a method to be retried if it fails, and Spring's `CircuitBreakerFactory` is used to protect against cascading failures by opening the circuit breaker when consecutive failures occur.

For OAuth 2.0 integration:
