["This is a complex task that would require many lines of code to accomplish. Here's an example of how you could structure such a modular JavaScript framework, but please keep in mind this is just a simplified representation and won't cover all your requirements fully:

```javascript
class FileLoader {
    constructor(dependencies = {}, cacheEnabled = true) {
        this.cache = new CacheManager();
        this.dependencies = dependencies;
        this.cacheEnabled = cacheEnabled;
    }

    async loadFile(url, callback) {
        let cachedContent = null;

        if (this.cacheEnabled) {
            cachedContent = await this.cache.getFromCache(url);
        }

        if (cachedContent !== null) {
            return Promise.resolve(callback ? callback(null, cachedContent) : cachedContent);
        } else {
            try {
                const response = await fetch(url);
                const content = await response.text();

                if (this.cacheEnabled) {
                    this.cache.addToCache(url, content);
                }

                return Promise.resolve(callback ? callback(null, content) : content);
            } catch (error) {
                return Promise.reject(callback ? callback(error, null) : error);
            }
        }
    }
}

class CacheManager {
    constructor() {
        this.cache = {};
    }

    getFromCache(url) {
        if (this.cache[url]) {
            return Promise.resolve(this.cache[url]);
        } else {
            return Promise.reject();
        }
    }

    addToCache(url, content) {
        this.cache[url] = content;
    }
}
```
In this example, we have a FileLoader class which can load files from local or remote sources and cache them for future use. It has an option to turn off caching if needed. We also have a CacheManager that provides basic getter and setter methods for the internal cache object.

