Here is the C function that logs an error message if a given condition is met:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdarg.h>

void log_error(const char* format, ...) {
    va_list args;
    va_start(args, format);

    // Allocate a buffer for the error message
    int len = vsnprintf(NULL, 0, format, args);
    if (len < 0) {
        return;
    }
    char* buffer = malloc((len + 1) * sizeof(*buffer));

    // Format the error message and write it to the log file
    vsnprintf(buffer, len + 1, format, args);
    FILE* logFile = fopen("error.log", "a");
    if (logFile != NULL) {
        fwrite(buffer, sizeof(*buffer), len, logFile);
        fclose(logFile);
    }

    free(buffer);
    va_end(args);
}
```

This function uses `vsnprintf` to format the error message and `fopen/fwrite/fclose` to write it to a log file. The error is appended to the log file, creating it if necessary. Note that proper error handling has been kept minimal for simplicity, and real-world usage would require more robust error checking and recovery mechanisms.
']

