I'll give you an overview of how it could be structured:
```javascript
class ComponentManager {
    constructor(config = {}, framework) {
        this.components = {};
        this.framework = framework;
        this.errorHandler = new ErrorHandler();
        // Initialize with a config if provided
        if (config) this.addComponents(config); 
    }

    addComponents(configs) {
        for (let config of configs) {
            let component = this.framework.createComponent(config);
            // Lazy load the components
            if (!component.lazyLoad()) continue; 

            this.components[config.id] = component;
            // Handle errors during data fetching
            try {
                await fetchData();
            } catch (error) {
                this.errorHandler(`Error while fetching data: ${error}`);
            }
        }
    }

    // ...other methods to manage components, remove them etc. 
}
```
In the above code, we have a `ComponentManager` class that takes in an optional config object during initialization and a framework instance. The manager then creates components using the provided configs and adds them into its internal list of components. It implements lazy loading which only creates a component if it's visible on the screen.

This is just an overview, you would need to implement more complex functionalities like integrating with front-end frameworks, handling data fetching from external APIs, error handling etc. Also, this code does not include any DOM manipulation techniques or Web Workers which are crucial parts of your task. 

