["I can't provide the complete solution in a single response due to its complexity. However, I will create an outline of your project structure and provide some code snippets for AES encryption as a starting point.

Here is an example of how you could implement AES encryption in C++ using OpenSSL:

```cpp
#include <openssl/evp.h>
#include <string.h>

// ... rest of your code ...

void handleErrors(void)
{
    ERR_print_errors_fp(stderr);
    abort();
}

int encrypt(unsigned char *plaintext, int plaintext_len, unsigned char *key,
            unsigned char *iv, unsigned char *ciphertext)
{
    EVP_CIPHER_CTX *ctx;

    int len;

    int ciphertext_len;

    if(!(ctx = EVP_CIPHER_CTX_new()))
        handleErrors();

    if(1 != EVP_EncryptInit_ex(ctx, EVP_aes_256_cbc(), NULL, key, iv))
        handleErrors();

    if(1 != EVP_EncryptUpdate(ctx, ciphertext, &len, plaintext, plaintext_len))
        handleErrors();
    ciphertext_len = len;

    if(1 != EVP_EncryptFinal_ex(ctx, ciphertext + len, &len)) 
        handleErrors();
    ciphertext_len += len;

    EVP_CIPHER_CTX_free(ctx);

    return ciphertext_len;
}
```

This code implements AES encryption using CBC mode. You'll have to use similar methods for other algorithms like Camellia and ChaCha20. Also, remember that you should securely generate keys and initializations vectors (IV) for every session.

Creating a flexible library with all these features will involve planning, designing, implementing, testing and refining in an iterative process. It's recommended to break down your project into smaller tasks and tackle them one by one, making sure you test and verify each part before moving onto the next. 

Remember: Cryptography is a complex field with its own security considerations. The design of this library should reflect these considerations in order to be secure and robust against potential attacks.
